<h2>India's single largest-value product export by our count</h2>
<p>Among every product we've verified trade data for, groundnut kernels represent the largest total export value — USD 823.2 million across roughly 730,152 MT in calendar 2024. And unlike many of our other product lines, this trade concentrates overwhelmingly in one region: Southeast Asia.</p>
<p>Indonesia alone imported USD 295.3 million, about 36% of the global total. Add Vietnam (USD 124.9 million), the Philippines (USD 70.3 million), Malaysia (USD 56.2 million) and Thailand (USD 53.9 million), and Southeast Asia accounts for roughly 73% of everything India exports in this category. If you're building a Southeast Asia sourcing relationship with India, groundnut kernels are arguably the single clearest anchor product available.</p>

<h2>Bold and Java: two different products</h2>
<p>India exports two main groundnut kernel types. Bold (runner-type) kernels are larger and are the more common export grade; Java (Spanish-type) kernels are smaller with a distinct flavour profile suited to different processing applications. Both are graded by count per ounce, moisture and oil content. Tell us your intended use — snack food processing, confectionery, oil extraction, direct retail — and we'll recommend the appropriate type and grade.</p>

<h2>Aflatoxin testing is not optional</h2>
<p>This deserves emphasis: groundnut is inherently one of the highest-risk commodities for aflatoxin contamination among all agricultural products, and this is true regardless of origin, not specific to Indian supply. EU and Japanese import limits are particularly strict and strictly enforced. We treat pre-shipment aflatoxin testing as standard practice for groundnut shipments rather than something to arrange only on request — {{ site.inspectionStatement | lower }}</p>

<h2>Indonesian import requirements</h2>
<p>Food products generally require registration with BPOM (Badan Pengawas Obat dan Makanan), Indonesia's food and drug regulator, before import — typically the importer's process, supported by documentation from our side. Halal certification is also frequently relevant given Indonesia's formalised system through BPJPH; your importer specifies the required certifying body and we match documentation accordingly.</p>

<h2>Why India competes here despite Southeast Asia's own production</h2>
<p>Indonesia, Vietnam and the Philippines all grow groundnut domestically, so Indian supply into this region reflects a genuine cost or volume advantage rather than a captive market. That's worth knowing as a buyer — it means Indian groundnut pricing is competing directly against regional alternatives, which keeps quotes honest on both sides.</p>
